<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Natal Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1843 CE – 1910 CE · capital Pietermaritzburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Britain's sugar colony on the Indian Ocean coast of South Africa, whose Indian labourers and segregation policies shaped later South Africa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/natalia-republic">Natalia Republic</a> in 1843 to keep the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> from a port of their own. Natal was first run as a district of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> and became a separate colony in 1856, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietermaritzburg">Pietermaritzburg</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> as its port.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilus-shepstone">Theophilus Shepstone</a>, Secretary for Native Affairs, set up reserves ruled by chiefs under customary law, a model for later segregation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar plantations</a> on the coast relied on indentured labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1860; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> began his political career in Natal's Indian community. Responsible government came in 1893, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zululand</a> was incorporated in 1897. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambatha-rebellion">Bambatha rebellion</a> against a poll tax was crushed in 1906.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Natal's English-speaking voters were wary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> dominance, but in 1910 the colony joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natal-province">Natal Province</a>. Merged with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">KwaZulu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> in 1994, it is now the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/natal-colony.jpg" alt="Natal Colony" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Natal_Colony_(1875%E2%80%931910).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1843 CE – 1910 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pietermaritzburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Theophilus Shepstone, John Robinson</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Zulu traditional religion, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, isiZulu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>South Africa</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Natal, Natal</dd></dl></aside></div>
